        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 836,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Gives the position of the smallest value in an array, ignoring any nan (\"not a number\") in it.\n\nnp.argmin treats nan as if it were the answer - it gives the position of the first nan - so a single dropped reading points at the gap instead of the real smallest value. np.nanargmin looks only at the values that are there.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.nanargmin <axis: int>\nDefault: axis None (the whole array, counted as if flattened into one list).\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input is searched and sent.\n\naxis (menu, also an inlet):\nNone searches every element. A number searches along that axis: for a 2-D array, 0 gives one position per column, 1 one per row.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nnanargmin:\nThe position: a single number, or one per column or row. If a whole row or column is nan there is no position to give, and nothing is sent.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n3 nan 7 1. np.nanargmin gives 3, the position of the 1. np.argmin gives 1 - the position of the nan. A metro re-sends the list twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.nanmin gives the smallest value itself. np.argmin does not skip gaps. np.nanargmax finds the other extreme.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 820,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Gives the position of the smallest value in an array, ignoring any nan (\"not a number\") in it.\n\nnp.argmin treats nan as if it were the answer - it gives the position of the first nan - so a single dropped reading points at the gap instead of the real smallest value. np.nanargmin looks only at the values that are there.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.nanargmin <axis: int>\nDefault: axis None (the whole array, counted as if flattened into one list).\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input is searched and sent.\n\naxis (menu, also an inlet):\nNone searches every element. A number searches along that axis: for a 2-D array, 0 gives one position per column, 1 one per row.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nnanargmin:\nThe position: a single number, or one per column or row. If a whole row or column is nan there is no position to give, and nothing is sent.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n3 nan 7 1. np.nanargmin gives 3, the position of the 1. np.argmin gives 1 - the position of the nan. A metro re-sends the list twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.nanmin gives the smallest value itself. np.argmin does not skip gaps. np.nanargmax finds the other extreme."
        },
